Platinum, after these KDE themes, in the style of Mac OS 9:

  Platinum Retro OS9, a window decoration and global theme by Niels (nielsvm),
  GNU General Public License, version 3 (LICENSE)
  https://store.kde.org/p/1319936
  https://store.kde.org/p/2361987

  QPlatinum, a colour scheme by zinjanthr0pus
  https://store.kde.org/p/1287747

  Mac9Kvantum, a Kvantum theme by drgordbord, a reference only
  https://store.kde.org/p/1766812

art/ holds the window decoration's artwork and settings as they were published.
The decorator here is written for Renku and Haiku, with colours and sizes measured
from that artwork and its buttons taken from it by tools/make-platinum-art.py.

The control look is drawn from scratch. Its colours and sizes were measured from
Mac9Kvantum, which has no licence, so nothing of its artwork is included. Should
its author license it, the look could be matched more closely.

These add-ons are under the GNU GPL, version 3 or later, unlike the rest of Power
Theme, which is under the MIT licence. Power Theme doesn't link them: app_server
and applications load them, like any other decorator and control look.

The theme's wallpaper comes with the global theme, under the same licence.

The theme's icons are in data/icons/NineIconsRedux: NineIcons Redux by aitees, under
the MIT licence (its LICENSE file is with them), copied by tools/make-nineicons.py.
It's based on Grassmunk's NineIcons, which has no licence of its own. 7 of the 156
files here are identical to that pack: application-x-executable, and some sizes of
folder, x-office-address-book and two OpenDocument template icons.

Not ported: the global theme's cursors, the classic panel layout, and the window buttons' help, keep above and shade buttons, which
Haiku's windows don't have. A decorator can't blend, so the frame's shadow, which is
translucent in the artwork, is a solid dark line. Scroll bars keep Haiku's usual
width, because its windows lay themselves out around it.

Apple is not affiliated with this project, or with the themes above.
